Now testPathExists() is fixed, so the recursive path order issue is solved.

The two remaining failures are both connected to the failed-path test:

Testing path that does not exist: FAIL
Testing repeated findPath clears old result: FAIL
That means your test is still probably using a path that actually exists, or the executable was not recompiled after editing.

Run these commands:

grep "findPath" mytest.cpp
Look for these two functions. They should use 3, 0, not 4, 0.

bool Tester::testPathDoesNotExist(){
    Graph graph("testdata.txt");

    if (graph.empty()){
        return false;
    }

    bool result = graph.findPath(3, 0);

    if (result){
        return false;
    }

    if (!graph.m_path.empty()){
        return false;
    }

    return true;
}
And:

bool Tester::testRepeatedFindPathClearsOldResult(){
    Graph graph("testdata.txt");

    if (graph.empty()){
        return false;
    }

    if (!graph.findPath(0, 15)){
        return false;
    }

    if (graph.m_path.empty()){
        return false;
    }

    if (graph.findPath(3, 0)){
        return false;
    }

    if (!graph.m_path.empty()){
        return false;
    }

    return true;
}
Then recompile, not just rerun:

g++ -Wall mytest.cpp graph.cpp -o mytest.out
./mytest.out
If it still fails after that, send your full mytest.cpp. At this point, graph.cpp is probably okay; the remaining issue is almost certainly in those two test functions or in testdata.txt.


